# Quote: Mario Bunge - Treatise on Basic Philosophy - Epistemology & Methodology III - Part II

> It has been held that the reason for this is that psychoanalysis is irrefutable (Popper 1963 pp. 35 fT.). Actually some psychoanalytic hypotheses are individually refutable and, moreover, have been refuted (Bunge 1967a, Grünbaum 1984). What is true is that any of them can be protected from refutation by further members of the psychoanalytic gang. Typical examples of such protectionist policy are the following: (a) every male has an Oedipus complex; if someone fails to exhibit it, this only proves that he has repressed the complex; (b) the critics of psychoanalysis do nothing but exhibit the resistance phenomenon: their attitude confirms the doctrine (e.g. Flax 1981); (c) "The fact that in the technique of psychoanalysis a means has been found by which the opposing force [resistance] can be removed and the ideas in question made conscious renders this theory irrefutable" (Freud 1962 p. 4).

